  "id": "Compound::DB01001 - Gene::154",
  "verbalization": "Salbutamol, a well-known adrenergic beta-2 agonist, is linked to Adrb2 through a series of molecular and pharmacological relationships. The evidence suggests that Salbutamol, as part of the broader class of adrenergic beta-2 agonists, binds to Adrb2 through a shared pathway involving phenylethanolamines. This is supported by the fact that multiple adrenergic beta-2 agonists, such as Pirbuterol and Formoterol, also bind to Adrb2, indicating a common mechanism of action. The lowest common ancestors of Salbutamol and these other agonists are both classified under the broader category of bronchodilators and organonitrogen compounds, highlighting a shared pharmacological profile. The integration of these pathways suggests that Salbutamol exerts its effects by activating Adrb2, likely through a mechanism involving the binding of phenylethanolamine derivatives, which is consistent across different adrenergic beta-2 agonists. This unified mechanism underscores the role of Adrb2 as a target for these compounds, reinforcing the pharmacological relationship between Salbutamol and Adrb2.",
